WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
(car rumbling)

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.200
(people chattering)

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.480
(boy speaks in foreign language)

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.720
(soldiers shout)
(whistle blows)

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.520
(people speak in foreign language)

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.280
(dramatic music)

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.320
- My name is Jonas Poher Rasmussen

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.200
and I'm the director of "Flee,"

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.480
an animated documentary

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.200
about a friend of mine
who I met when I was 15

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.960
and a physical fight going
from Afghanistan to Denmark.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.320
But it's even more so
about an internal fight,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.240
about a man who has had to
flee himself all his life.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.000
When he was a young man in Afghanistan

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.120
he couldn't be openly gay,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.040
so he kinda had to hide that away.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.360
And then he arrived in Denmark

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.280
and all of a sudden he
had to hide his past.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.000
And also it's a story
about memory and trauma.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.120
(dramatic music)

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.880
(clapperboard clacks)

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.400
The scene we're about to go into

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.800
is from the end of the film,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.160
and at this point he came out
with his story about his past,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.760
he told us about how he came
from Afghanistan to Denmark,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.360
and just afterwards now
he has to tell his family

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.440
about him being gay and they accept him.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.960
All of a sudden everything is out

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.000
and so the celebration at the gay bar

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.640
is really about him finally
being in a place in the world

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.680
where he can be who he is,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:10.800
you know, with his sexuality,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.360
but also with his past.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.920
(chain clinks)

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.880
(woman speaks in foreign language)

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.480
(Amin speaks in foreign language)

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.720
(woman speaks in foreign language)

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.320
(Amin speaks in foreign language)

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.720
- [Jonas] So this is a good sequence

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.920
where Amin is asking for
medicine for being gay.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.200
You know, he doesn't see it as a disease

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.640
but he thought it was
something that you could cure,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.280
and actually Amin was really embarrassed

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.640
by this part in the film

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.800
and asked me if that
needed to be in there,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.160
so we had a long conversation about that

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.200
'cause I thought it was so
important to have that in

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.680
because to kind of really
understand where he came from

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.240
and how suppressed his sexuality was.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.440
(woman speaks in foreign language)

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.760
- [Jonas] The lady who he's talking to

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.200
worked at this asylum center.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.160
Here the voice is
actually made by my wife.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.440
(woman speaks in foreign language)

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.560
(Amin speaks in foreign language)

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.640
- She was right next to me

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.720
so I asked her if she could do the lines,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:12.000
and they actually kind
of look alike as well.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.680
So the animation style we chose for "Flee"

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.040
was really being as authentic as possible,

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.200
so it's really inspired by the landscapes

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.680
from just around the area where I grew up.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.680
We recognize the fields
and the farms and hills.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.640
But it was really about
trying to support his voice,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.560
we were listening carefully,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.840
and do a lot of research.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.840
And here it's Denmark,
close to my hometown.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.520
We did like a big art bible

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.680
of powerful references from
different visual artists.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.800
So one was Edward Hopper.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.080
There's this certain sense
of solitude in his images

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.360
that we really wanted to have in there

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.640
'cause Amin's story is really a story

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.480
about solitude as well,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.160
about loneliness, about not
being able to be honest.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.480
And we felt that in
Edward Hopper's paintings,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.440
that there is this sense of solitude.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.160
(Amin speaks in foreign language)

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.080
(woman speaks in foreign language)

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.440
- [Jonas] Here he kinda started realizing

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.400
at some point I would
have to tell my family,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.000
and he was quite certain

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.480
that they wouldn't be able to accept it

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.000
because it wasn't
acceptable in Afghanistan

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.640
and in the culture in general.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.120
So this sequence really
plays on, you know,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.640
his own prejudice against his own family

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.240
and I think most other people's prejudices

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.320
against how gay people are received

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.360
in Muslim families and countries.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.840
And I really wanted to have that in there,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.480
you know, the sense of okay
but this is not gonna go well.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.680
We worked in this software in animation

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:40.480
that's called Shotgun,

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.560
so every shot has a number and a name,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.240
and then the animating director
who did the artwork and me

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.720
we would each kind of of write

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.560
what's going on in every shot.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.880
So in a shot like this one

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.560
I would kind of write where
the family is heading.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.680
It's the first time
they're together for years

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.400
and we should feel kind of the comfort

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.040
of him being with his family

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.680
but also quite fast feel

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.920
that he's annoyed with his older brother.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.880
Here he's teasing and here Amin is afraid

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.560
and annoyed at the same time.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.240
And then the animating
director could use that

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.520
for the direction for the animators

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.720
to kind of make it look like it.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.440
So, of course, when we see
how his brother looks at him

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.440
afterwards he said that he's
not interested in girls,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.480
that look is because of his fear,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.120
again, it's because of Amin's prejudice

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.120
and what he thinks his brother thinks,

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.520
and we wanted to have that in there.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:35.720
In this specific scene,

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.960
we zoom in on Amin, he's in the car,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:42.640
and he is realizing that now
I said it, now it's goodbye,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.560
I have to live my life from
now on without my family.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.640
So here we kind of decided
to have these flashbacks

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.880
and he would start to
recall everything in detail.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.240
Using these cinematic tools,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.680
using that pull and stretch,

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.840
like really stretch time at times

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.360
so you feel okay now time stands still

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.360
because this is a crucial moment,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.560
and really using that
and feel the pressure

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.200
that he was under

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.400
because he carried this
thing around that he was gay

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.920
and he didn't think that his family

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.080
would be able to accept it.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.560
So in order to give the audience

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.080
just a little bit of sense of relief,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.960
of that sense of relief that he felt

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.280
when he finally came out

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.160
and he understood that they
actually did accept him,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.400
and when Amin walks down
the corridor towards the bar

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:30.400
he himself knows that it's
not a brothel or a strip club,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.280
but we wanted to keep the information

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.080
that the family had accepted him

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.400
until the very last second.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.920
(gentle music)

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:41.000
When I write, I always listen to music

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.320
and I have different playlists.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.000
Often, it's non-verbal music,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.000
like electronic, classical,
different kinds of music.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.760
And as I was kind of transcribing

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.080
and working on this sequence

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.000
I was listening to a playlist

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.720
and this Daft Punk song came on.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.680
That song really starts
when really on the moment

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.800
where you get this sense of solitude,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.640
that now he's on his own,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.080
and he's kind of saying goodbye

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.840
to all the good things he had,

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.920
all the support he had from his family

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.800
and all the good memories he had.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.280
And it just felt like a perfect
fit from the very beginning

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.320
and I had this kind of feeling of okay

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.080
this needs to be in there

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.800
because it had this sense of loneliness,

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.360
and this sequence is really about

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.000
Amin feeling all alone in the world

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.600
and this song has this
kind of shift as well

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.560
where it kind of turns
into more of a party song.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.960
(gentle music)
(people cheering)

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.640
(upbeat music)

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.440
So it just felt like the perfect match

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.200
and then it was just the struggle

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.560
of getting the rights to use it,

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.360
and I just kind of hunted down
Daft Punk for three years,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.240
I think they must have been
really annoyed at the end.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.920
But in the end they saw
the clip and loved it.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.400
I think it was one of the biggest release

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.680
of making this film, actually,

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.840
to get that song.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.600
(people cheering)
(upbeat music)

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.160
(bartender speaks in foreign language)

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.880
- [Jonas] And the gay
bar is really inspired

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.000
by some of the gay bars we went to,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.800
and specifically one in Copenhagen,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.080
but also the specific one that he went to.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.280
So it's kind of a mix
of different gay bars

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.880
but it's really for me inspired by

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.280
a lot of the parties we went to together

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.040
and by a lot of gay bars
we went to together.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.880
And of course the bartender
is a little bit inspired

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.720
by Jean-Claude Van Damme

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.480
because I wanted to add
his lifetime crush in there

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.520
just to kind of show that it was okay

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.640
for him to feel attracted

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:27.080
to someone like Jean-Claude Van Damme.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.040
(people cheering)

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.200
(Amin speaks in foreign language)

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.280
(Amin laughs)

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.240
- [Jonas] In the very last
part of this sequence,

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:41.840
we see him disappear in a crowd

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.800
and finally he is in a place
in the world, you know,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.360
where he is safe, he doesn't
have to flee anymore,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.200
and he can be openly gay,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.560
and it really concludes the film

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.800
about this guy who's had
to run away from things

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.120
all of his life

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.240
and now he can be who he is.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.680
And that's kind of where, you know,

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.000
the main theme of the film ends.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.000
This story is really about
sharing and listening

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.400
and the healing powers of sharing,

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.160
and it was just a slow process

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.680
of kind of going deeper and
deeper into these memories,

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:19.120
and then of course choosing
the parts that worked

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.160
because this story expands
for more than 30 years,

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.440
so it was really about finding, okay,

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:23.600
but what's the core of this?

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:24.920
And then trying to figuring out

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.200
what part of his testimony
needs to be in the film

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.800
and how these experiences
have marked him for life.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.040
And then finding archival footage

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:36.520
and finding a style of animating
that could support that.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.720
But all the time in the core was

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.320
listening to the voice,
listening to what was being said,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.920
really listening carefully
to how he said things,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.080
and make sure that we
tried to support that

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.520
as good as we could.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.120
(gentle music)

